00:04:11.820 --> 00:04:13.140
Here are the results of that question!

00:04:13.140 --> 00:04:15.650
Total

00:04:13.140 --> 00:04:15.650
Multiple
Answers

00:04:13.140 --> 00:04:15.650
Ohkura | Ashiya | Shibuya | Shibata | Mikuriya | Koizumi | Koshiyama | Sonohara | Hirosue

00:04:15.650 --> 00:04:17.400
<b>Koshiyama</b>

00:04:17.880 --> 00:04:20.590
<i>I'm starting off in first 
place alongside Ohkura-san!</i>

00:04:21.120 --> 00:04:22.160
The correct answers are:

00:04:22.160 --> 00:04:27.910
the liver, the heart, the spleen, the lung, 
the kidney, the stomach, the large intestine,

00:04:27.910 --> 00:04:31.290
the small intestine, the gall bladder, 
the urinary bladder, and the san jiao.

00:04:32.080 --> 00:04:33.220
What's "the san jiao"?

00:04:33.220 --> 00:04:34.840
Never heard of it.

00:04:34.840 --> 00:04:39.680
"Of the zang-fu organs, which is the 
only one that isn't actually an organ?"

00:04:40.050 --> 00:04:43.550
"The answer is the san jiao." 
It's a well-known question.

00:04:43.850 --> 00:04:46.960
It's easy to get the zang-fu organs wrong,

00:04:46.960 --> 00:04:51.810
because there are so many 
organs to choose from.

00:04:52.170 --> 00:04:55.720
If even one wrong answer is given in the 
quiz to determine your initial score,

00:04:55.720 --> 00:04:57.240
you get zero points.

00:04:56.020 --> 00:04:58.360
Ashiya | Shibuya | Shibata | Mikuriya | Koizumi | Koshiyama | Sonohara

00:04:59.730 --> 00:05:02.140
<i>Mikuriya-kun got zero points?</i>

00:05:02.670 --> 00:05:03.730
Zero points!

00:05:04.480 --> 00:05:06.770
And you're supposed to be a quiz freak!

00:05:07.880 --> 00:05:08.700
Akira-kun...

00:05:09.010 --> 00:05:11.830
It's not nice to laugh at 
other people's mistakes.

00:05:12.410 --> 00:05:14.000
What's this, Koshiyama?

00:05:14.000 --> 00:05:16.580
Feeling confident because 
you've got an early lead?

00:05:18.160 --> 00:05:20.920
Starting with zero points? Fine by me.

00:05:21.850 --> 00:05:23.000
I'll show all of you.

00:05:23.570 --> 00:05:28.260
We will now start the fastest finger 
board quiz from these initial scores.

00:05:29.840 --> 00:05:30.700
Question.

00:05:31.050 --> 00:05:33.800
The first manned space flight 
was completed by Gaga—

00:05:34.300 --> 00:05:34.850
<i>That was fast!</i>

00:05:35.220 --> 00:05:37.560
The person who buzzed was Mikuriya-kun!

00:05:37.870 --> 00:05:40.700
<i>A wrong answer after buzzing 
means a loss of three points.</i>

00:05:40.700 --> 00:05:43.610
<i>He could be disqualified! 
How could he buzz in so soon?</i>

00:05:44.260 --> 00:05:46.330
If you answer correctly after buzzing in,

00:05:46.330 --> 00:05:48.550
you can add your points to your own score

00:05:48.550 --> 00:05:50.530
or subtract them from another player.

00:05:50.530 --> 00:05:52.280
The choice is yours.

00:05:52.560 --> 00:05:54.500
What will Mikuriya-kun do?

00:05:54.890 --> 00:05:57.250
Now, everyone, show your boards!

00:05:57.250 --> 00:05:58.920
<b>McCandless</b>

00:05:57.250 --> 00:05:59.710
<b>Leonov</b>

00:05:57.490 --> 00:05:59.710
They're all rather divided on the answer.

00:05:58.920 --> 00:05:59.710
<b>Tereshkova</b>

00:05:59.710 --> 00:06:02.420
<b>Akiyama Toyohiro</b>

00:05:59.710 --> 00:06:02.420
<b>Moon</b>

00:05:59.850 --> 00:06:03.050
And Mikuriya-kun, the one 
who buzzed in, said...

00:06:02.420 --> 00:06:06.380
<b>Alan Shepard</b>

00:06:03.460 --> 00:06:04.670
Alan Shepard.

00:06:04.670 --> 00:06:06.380
He was the only player to answer correctly!

00:06:06.730 --> 00:06:10.560
The first manned space flight 
was completed by Gagarin.

00:06:10.560 --> 00:06:13.390
The second was completed by 
which American astronaut?

00:06:13.700 --> 00:06:16.100
The correct answer is Alan Shepard.

00:06:16.540 --> 00:06:19.170
The emphasis was on "first"?

00:06:19.170 --> 00:06:21.200
It was barely noticeable.

00:06:21.200 --> 00:06:23.720
That's the perfect difficulty 
level for this round.

00:06:23.720 --> 00:06:25.360
And you buzzed in based on that?

00:06:25.720 --> 00:06:29.980
If someone else beat me to it and subtracted 
points from me, I'd be out anyway.

00:06:29.980 --> 00:06:32.070
And if I'm going to get 
knocked out regardless,

00:06:32.540 --> 00:06:34.330
I'd rather be the one responsible for it.

00:06:35.630 --> 00:06:36.790
Now, Mikuriya-kun,

00:06:37.250 --> 00:06:41.260
please decide how to distribute your four 
points, including your extra bonus point.

00:06:41.260 --> 00:06:42.250
I'll keep them all.

00:06:42.250 --> 00:06:43.710
<b>Mikuriya</b>

00:06:43.970 --> 00:06:46.450
Dude, that first-year 
from Miyaura is amazing.

00:06:46.450 --> 00:06:48.220
He's already over ten points.

00:06:48.670 --> 00:06:50.430
<i>What am I doing?</i>

00:06:50.870 --> 00:06:54.610
<i>What made me think for a second that I could 
beat players who are leagues ahead of me</i>

00:06:54.610 --> 00:06:56.670
<i>in both knowledge and technique?</i>

00:06:58.560 --> 00:06:59.890
<i>I have to calm down.</i>

00:07:01.880 --> 00:07:04.110
<i>Don't force anything, and listen 
closely to the questions.</i>

00:07:04.110 --> 00:07:06.120
<i>I have to get some answers right!</i>

00:07:09.390 --> 00:07:10.200
Question.

00:07:14.700 --> 00:07:17.540
O, Romeo, Romeo—

00:07:17.820 --> 00:07:19.160
That was me!

00:07:21.600 --> 00:07:22.830
<i>What was that?!</i>

00:07:22.990 --> 00:07:24.860
Well, that scene recreation was a total flop.

00:07:24.860 --> 00:07:25.870
Yikes...

00:07:25.870 --> 00:07:27.550
What was Yukicchi doing?

00:07:27.550 --> 00:07:30.530
I wanted to do more of <i>Romeo and Juliet</i>!

00:07:30.530 --> 00:07:32.130
Curse you, Akira!

00:07:32.830 --> 00:07:34.600
All right, show your boards!

00:07:34.600 --> 00:07:38.060
<b>Romeo and Juliet</b>

00:07:35.190 --> 00:07:38.860
"O, Romeo, Romeo, wherefore art thou Romeo?"

00:07:37.140 --> 00:07:38.060
<b>Verona</b>

00:07:37.220 --> 00:07:38.060
<b>Romeo and Juliet</b>

00:07:38.860 --> 00:07:43.480
is a famous line that appears 
in which Shakespeare tragedy?

00:07:39.060 --> 00:07:40.770
<b>Romeo and Juliet</b>

00:07:42.190 --> 00:07:43.480
<b>Capulet</b>

00:07:42.480 --> 00:07:43.480
<b>Romeo and Juliet</b>

00:07:42.730 --> 00:07:43.480
<b>Romeo and Juliet</b>

00:07:43.480 --> 00:07:46.570
<b>Romeo and Juliet</b>

00:07:43.760 --> 00:07:46.760
The answer is <i>Romeo and Juliet</i>.

00:07:46.760 --> 00:07:49.550
Sonohara-kun, please distribute 
your three points.

00:07:49.550 --> 00:07:50.990
Okay, then...

00:07:51.400 --> 00:07:54.030
Subtract two points from the squinting guy!

00:07:54.030 --> 00:07:54.840
What?!

00:07:55.100 --> 00:07:56.120
Ka-boom!

00:07:55.100 --> 00:07:56.120
<b>Ka-boom!</b>

00:07:56.120 --> 00:07:59.620
<b>Koizumi</b>

00:07:56.430 --> 00:07:59.620
K-Koizumi kun has a negative 
score, which means he's disqualified.

00:08:00.960 --> 00:08:02.180
You demon...

00:08:02.180 --> 00:08:04.630
Who should I subtract the other point from?

00:08:08.920 --> 00:08:10.980
I know! Boss Gorilla!

00:08:10.980 --> 00:08:12.340
You cocky little punk!

00:08:13.200 --> 00:08:15.090
Well, those are the rules.

00:08:16.070 --> 00:08:17.510
President Sonohara!

00:08:19.030 --> 00:08:22.480
I'll make this game 
interesting for all of you.

00:08:22.720 --> 00:08:24.850
W-Well, I'll take over from here.

00:08:24.850 --> 00:08:27.790
With Sonohara-kun's subtraction of points,

00:08:27.790 --> 00:08:29.550
Koizumi-kun is out,

00:08:29.550 --> 00:08:32.220
and Ohkura-kun's score is
 reduced to fifteen points.

00:08:32.220 --> 00:08:34.450
He's no longer one question 
away from a possible win.

00:08:34.830 --> 00:08:37.770
What was Asagaoka thinking when 
they made up these rules?

00:08:39.460 --> 00:08:41.500
I guess siblings do think alike.

00:08:41.730 --> 00:08:47.190
<i>I only wanted to make things exciting 
by adding an element of negotiation...</i>

00:08:47.190 --> 00:08:48.760
Everybody, shut up!

00:08:49.870 --> 00:08:52.600
Rules about subtracting points 
aren't that uncommon.

00:08:52.960 --> 00:08:55.720
Players can play however they 
want if it's within the rules!

00:08:57.220 --> 00:09:00.310
The only thing that bothers 
me is this kid's attitude.

00:09:00.310 --> 00:09:01.350
Who are you calling a gorilla?!

00:09:01.590 --> 00:09:03.040
Oops. Sorry.

00:09:03.040 --> 00:09:04.560
I take back the gorilla thing.

00:09:04.910 --> 00:09:05.620
Good.

00:09:06.720 --> 00:09:08.490
And that's okay with you, Ohkura?

00:09:08.490 --> 00:09:12.220
I don't want the regular meet to be 
sullied by this kind of quibbling.

00:09:12.840 --> 00:09:14.610
Yeah... I guess you're right.

00:09:14.930 --> 00:09:17.480
Ohkura's so grown-up.

00:09:17.480 --> 00:09:19.280
And he's younger than me...

00:09:19.280 --> 00:09:21.950
I hope I grow up to be that mature.

00:09:22.340 --> 00:09:23.440
Question.

00:09:23.440 --> 00:09:24.660
In the Catholic Church—

00:09:26.290 --> 00:09:27.370
Total

00:09:26.290 --> 00:09:27.370
Multiple
Answers

00:09:26.290 --> 00:09:32.000
Ohkura | Ashiya | Shibuya | Shibata | Mikuriya | Koizumi | Koshiyama | Sonohara | Hirosue

00:09:26.710 --> 00:09:28.010
Everyone answered wrong.

00:09:28.010 --> 00:09:30.300
Sonohara-kun loses three points.

00:09:30.940 --> 00:09:34.080
Why is Sonohara like that?

00:09:34.080 --> 00:09:35.910
He said he'd make the game interesting,

00:09:35.910 --> 00:09:37.890
but he's just messing it 
up for everyone else!

00:09:38.150 --> 00:09:40.480
But he's down to two points.

00:09:40.480 --> 00:09:42.850
I hope he'll settle down now.

00:09:44.360 --> 00:09:46.560
The correct answer is <i>Utakata no Ki</i>.

00:09:46.560 --> 00:09:49.270
Ohkura-kun and Koshiyama-kun 
both answered correctly.

00:09:49.570 --> 00:09:52.210
Yes! That's from Ougai's German trilogy!

00:09:52.210 --> 00:09:54.070
Woohoo! Way to go, boss!

00:09:54.070 --> 00:09:55.600
Don't call me "boss," either!

00:09:55.600 --> 00:09:58.490
Ohkura-kun, please distribute 
your three points.

00:09:58.760 --> 00:10:02.000
Yes... You want to be 
disqualified, don't you, kid?

00:10:02.000 --> 00:10:02.950
Huh?

00:10:03.070 --> 00:10:06.290
<i>If he loses three points, Akira-kun is out.</i>

00:10:09.210 --> 00:10:12.130
Making that face at me... won't help.

00:10:13.650 --> 00:10:15.660
I'll keep them all for myself.

00:10:17.470 --> 00:10:20.430
Ohkura-kun gets three points, 
taking him to eighteen.

00:10:20.430 --> 00:10:22.890
Total

00:10:20.430 --> 00:10:22.890
<b>Ohkura</b>

00:10:20.730 --> 00:10:22.890
He needs two more points to win.

00:10:23.200 --> 00:10:25.600
<i>I'm still two points behind Mikuriya-kun.</i>

00:10:25.960 --> 00:10:27.020
<i>I can't lose!</i>

00:10:28.110 --> 00:10:29.130
Question.

00:10:29.130 --> 00:10:31.200
American entertainment 
industry awards include

00:10:31.200 --> 00:10:33.400
the Emmy Award, Grammy 
Award, Academy Award—

00:10:34.320 --> 00:10:35.190
<i>I buzzed too late!</i>

00:10:35.600 --> 00:10:37.010
Shibuya-kun buzzed in!

00:10:37.950 --> 00:10:39.910
You finally got one, Shibuya!

00:10:40.600 --> 00:10:44.990
<i>If there are four awards, I should've 
buzzed on the "A" of "Academy," but...</i>

00:10:46.110 --> 00:10:48.120
This is a curious question.

00:10:48.590 --> 00:10:50.260
Why's that, Gakuto?

00:10:50.260 --> 00:10:52.900
Isn't it just a regular numerated question?

00:10:52.900 --> 00:10:54.720
No, probably not.

00:10:55.190 --> 00:10:57.920
Will Koshiyama notice the difference?

00:10:58.760 --> 00:11:01.010
Now, show your boards.

00:11:01.470 --> 00:11:02.840
Here's the rest of the question:

00:11:02.840 --> 00:11:05.880
What's the term used when 
someone who wins an Emmy,

00:11:05.880 --> 00:11:08.160
a Grammy, an Academy Award, and a Tony?

00:11:08.510 --> 00:11:10.980
The answer is "Showbiz Grand Slam."

00:11:11.170 --> 00:11:12.230
I knew it!

00:11:12.230 --> 00:11:22.360
<b>Showbiz Grand Slam</b>

00:11:12.680 --> 00:11:18.680
"EGOT" means Emmy, Grammy, Oscar, 
and Tony: the four initials.

00:11:18.680 --> 00:11:20.270
That's also correct!

00:11:20.270 --> 00:11:22.360
Lucky me! I saw that on TV!

00:11:22.640 --> 00:11:24.700
What was that all about?

00:11:24.700 --> 00:11:26.250
If it had been a numerical question,

00:11:26.250 --> 00:11:30.390
the award names would have 
been intonated separately.

00:11:27.830 --> 00:11:35.960
<b>Numerical Straight</b>

00:11:27.830 --> 00:11:38.300
<b>Q: American entertainment industry awards include the Emmy Award, 
Grammy Award, Academy Award, and what, when won along with 
the other three, constitutes a "Showbiz Grand Slam"?  A: Tony Award</b>

00:11:27.830 --> 00:11:38.300
<b>Straight</b>

00:11:27.830 --> 00:11:38.300
<b>Q: American entertainment industry awards include 
the Emmy Award, Grammy Award, Academy Award, and 
Tony Award, and winning all four of these awards 
is known as what?  A: Showbiz Grand Slam/EGOT</b>

00:11:30.390 --> 00:11:34.880
But the way she read out "Emmy Award" and 
"Grammy Award" made them flow together.

00:11:34.880 --> 00:11:38.300
So that's why he was slow to buzz in.

00:11:38.300 --> 00:11:41.180
<i>Whew, good! I was able to discern 
the difference in the reading!</i>

00:11:41.660 --> 00:11:45.510
<i>He had such a hard time 
at the Sekigawata meet,</i>

00:11:46.110 --> 00:11:47.140
<i>but now, he's...</i>

00:11:47.680 --> 00:11:50.390
Total

00:11:47.680 --> 00:11:50.390
Multiple
Answers

00:11:47.680 --> 00:11:50.390
Ohkura | Ashiya | Shibuya | Shibata | Mikuriya | Koizumi | Koshiyama | Sonohara | Hirosue

00:11:49.760 --> 00:11:51.640
So now, I have three points, huh?

00:11:50.020 --> 00:11:51.640
<b>Koshiyama | Sonohara | Hirosue</b>

00:11:52.560 --> 00:11:53.430
Question.

00:11:54.430 --> 00:11:55.400
In the Old Testament—

00:11:57.640 --> 00:12:00.820
U-Um... No one answered correctly.

00:12:01.560 --> 00:12:02.870
Not again!

00:12:02.870 --> 00:12:06.330
He's deliberately blocking the questions 
that everyone came prepared for!

00:12:06.540 --> 00:12:08.360
He's treating this like a joke...

00:12:08.360 --> 00:12:11.000
But you won't be killing any more questions.

00:12:08.790 --> 00:12:11.000
<b>Sonohara</b>

00:12:11.230 --> 00:12:13.460
Huh? "Killing questions"?

00:12:14.700 --> 00:12:17.920
That's not what I'm doing. 
Don't make it sound so awful.

00:12:19.880 --> 00:12:21.840
Total

00:12:19.880 --> 00:12:21.840
Multiple
Answers

00:12:19.880 --> 00:12:21.840
Ohkura | Ashiya | Shibuya | Shibata | Mikuriya | Koizumi | Koshiyama | Sonohara | Hirosue

00:12:22.160 --> 00:12:24.970
They're sounding bloodthirsty again...

00:12:24.970 --> 00:12:28.810
D-Don't worry. The next
question will calm the air.

00:12:29.120 --> 00:12:30.230
Question.

00:12:30.230 --> 00:12:34.810
In photography, placing one or 
both hands on one's own cheeks—

00:12:35.260 --> 00:12:37.020
<i>He buzzed again?!</i>

00:12:37.470 --> 00:12:40.360
Please demonstrate the pose 
as you give your answer.

00:12:40.830 --> 00:12:43.990
Now, everyone... pose!

00:12:45.530 --> 00:12:48.950
There it is! Embarrassing them by making 
them act out the answer worked!

00:12:49.170 --> 00:12:50.660
Y-You people...

00:12:50.920 --> 00:12:53.160
You just wanted to make us 
do this pose, didn't you?

00:12:53.520 --> 00:12:54.540
The rest of the question was:

00:12:54.540 --> 00:12:57.670
"...results in a pose that looks like your
 tooth hurts, resulting in what name?"

00:12:57.910 --> 00:13:00.170
The answer is the "toothache pose."

00:13:00.170 --> 00:13:05.630
<b>"Dazai" Pose</b>

00:13:00.170 --> 00:13:05.630
<b>Toothache Pose</b>

00:13:00.850 --> 00:13:04.060
Koshiyama, what's that you wrote? 
Putting your chin in your hands?

00:13:04.060 --> 00:13:05.630
This isn't the pose named after Dazai Osamu?!

00:13:05.810 --> 00:13:07.590
It's not this, either?!

00:13:07.590 --> 00:13:10.350
S-See? They're all much happier now!

00:13:10.350 --> 00:13:11.560
Y-Yes!

00:13:12.790 --> 00:13:13.630
The gori—

00:13:13.630 --> 00:13:16.150
I mean, the boss loses three points!

00:13:16.350 --> 00:13:17.900
<b>Shock</b>

00:13:16.670 --> 00:13:17.900
What?!

00:13:17.900 --> 00:13:20.360
Total

00:13:17.900 --> 00:13:20.360
Multiple
Answers

00:13:17.900 --> 00:13:20.360
Ohkura | Ashiya | Shibuya | Shibata | Mikuriya | Koizumi | Koshiyama | Sonohara | Hirosue

00:13:18.520 --> 00:13:20.950
<i>We probably won't get any more 
demonstrative questions for a while.</i>

00:13:20.950 --> 00:13:22.500
<i>He should settle down.</i>

00:13:23.270 --> 00:13:24.320
<i>And...</i>

00:13:27.160 --> 00:13:29.940
The correct answer is 
"Delight at Johor Bahru."

00:13:29.940 --> 00:13:32.330
Ashiya-kun has added three 
points to his own score.

00:13:32.330 --> 00:13:37.790
<b>Delight at Johor Bahru</b>

00:13:32.330 --> 00:13:37.790
<b>Delight at Johor Bahru</b>

00:13:32.330 --> 00:13:37.790
<b>Malaysia</b>

00:13:32.570 --> 00:13:36.760
Ohkura-kun has surpassed 20 points, 
making him the first to win!

00:13:37.920 --> 00:13:39.790
<i>Ohkura-san really is amazing.</i>

00:13:41.500 --> 00:13:44.120
Let's play again sometime, boss!

00:13:44.120 --> 00:13:44.970
I don't think so!

00:13:44.970 --> 00:13:47.930
Total

00:13:44.970 --> 00:13:47.930
Multiple
Answers

00:13:44.970 --> 00:13:47.930
Ohkura | Ashiya | Shibuya | Shibata | Mikuriya | Koizumi | Koshiyama | Sonohara | Hirosue

00:13:45.200 --> 00:13:47.930
Only two more can advance to the next round.

00:13:47.930 --> 00:13:50.640
Currently, Ashiya-kun has eighteen points,

00:13:50.640 --> 00:13:52.850
Koshiyama-kun has sixteen points,

00:13:52.850 --> 00:13:55.310
and Mikuriya-kun is close 
behind with fifteen points.

00:13:55.630 --> 00:13:56.650
Question.

00:13:56.650 --> 00:13:58.410
Facing backwards to throw a coin—

00:13:58.900 --> 00:13:59.900
Yes!

00:13:59.900 --> 00:14:01.110
<i>Damn, he's fast!</i>

00:14:01.110 --> 00:14:02.860
<b>Trevi Fountain</b>

00:14:01.110 --> 00:14:02.860
<b>Trevi Fountain</b>

00:14:01.470 --> 00:14:04.270
The correct answer is the 
Trevi Fountain. Well done!

00:14:02.860 --> 00:14:05.860
<b>Q: Facing backwards to throw a coin into the water is said 
to make one's wish come true at which fountain in Rome 
designed by Nicola Salvi?  A: The Trevi Fountain</b>

00:14:04.270 --> 00:14:06.740
Ashiya-kun has reached 20 points!

00:14:06.740 --> 00:14:09.210
Who will you subtract the 
remaining point from?

00:14:09.590 --> 00:14:11.760
I'm forced to subtract a point, huh?

00:14:11.760 --> 00:14:12.580
Let's see...

00:14:13.110 --> 00:14:16.290
<i>The one I should subtract from 
is the one I see as a threat.</i>

00:14:17.610 --> 00:14:18.490
Mikuriya.

00:14:19.840 --> 00:14:22.460
You're the one who said we 
should show no mercy, right?

00:14:23.000 --> 00:14:24.420
That's right.

00:14:24.420 --> 00:14:27.880
Ashiya-kun takes a point from 
his own teammate, Mikuriya-kun!

00:14:28.540 --> 00:14:32.600
<i>So it doesn't even matter if you're from 
the same school, or what year you're in.</i>

00:14:33.040 --> 00:14:35.770
<i>They both recognize each other as rivals.</i>

00:14:36.640 --> 00:14:39.500
<i>He took that point because 
he recognized his talent.</i>

00:14:39.910 --> 00:14:43.170
<i>I wonder if that feels more 
satisfying or frustrating...</i>

00:14:43.170 --> 00:14:44.780
<i>What must it feel like?</i>

00:14:45.000 --> 00:14:48.940
<i>You told me to do quiz bowl, and 
said you'd take revenge on me.</i>

00:14:49.280 --> 00:14:52.570
<i>I want the kind of heated battle with 
you that'll make my heart thump.</i>

00:14:53.540 --> 00:14:54.490
Mikuriya-kun!

00:14:56.450 --> 00:15:00.580
I'm going to get revenge for 
losing to Sekigawata now!

00:15:05.920 --> 00:15:06.710
<b>Gakuto-senpai</b>

00:15:07.210 --> 00:15:07.920
<b>Touched</b>

00:15:07.920 --> 00:15:08.880
Ohkura

00:15:07.920 --> 00:15:08.920
<b>Happy 
birthday,
 Sasajima!!</b>

00:15:07.920 --> 00:15:09.010
<b>Congrats</b>

00:15:09.930 --> 00:15:12.210
Wow, he's declared war!

00:15:12.210 --> 00:15:13.800
You can do it, Koshiyama-kun!

00:15:14.420 --> 00:15:15.260
Too late for that.

00:15:15.890 --> 00:15:18.270
Our revenge match has already started.

00:15:18.630 --> 00:15:19.980
Y-Yeah!

00:15:20.650 --> 00:15:24.870
Koshiyama-kun, with seventeen points, can 
advance if he buzzes and answers correctly here.

00:15:24.870 --> 00:15:26.610
Who will buzz in first?

00:15:26.950 --> 00:15:28.570
Will we see a comeback 
from one of the others?

00:15:29.280 --> 00:15:30.110
Question.

00:15:30.740 --> 00:15:34.990
Meaning "governing the nation and saving 
its people," the word for "economy—

00:15:34.990 --> 00:15:35.620
Yes!

00:15:35.620 --> 00:15:37.450
<b>Keisei Saimin</b>

00:15:35.620 --> 00:15:37.450
<b>Keisei Saimin</b>

00:15:35.620 --> 00:15:40.040
<b>Keisei Saimin</b>

00:15:36.190 --> 00:15:38.170
"Keisei saimin" is correct!

00:15:37.750 --> 00:15:40.120
<b>Q: Meaning "governing the nation and saving its people," the word for "economy" 
in Japanese originated from what four-syllable idiom?  A: Keisei saimin</b>

00:15:38.170 --> 00:15:40.870
Shibata-kun of Kaijou is making a comeback!

00:15:41.310 --> 00:15:42.470
Question.

00:15:42.470 --> 00:15:47.380
In the process of scientific experiments, 
the escape of harmful organisms—

00:15:47.560 --> 00:15:48.760
Mikuriya-kun buzzed in!

00:15:49.760 --> 00:15:52.180
Total

00:15:49.760 --> 00:15:52.180
Multiple
Answers

00:15:49.760 --> 00:15:52.180
Ohkura | Ashiya | Shibuya | Shibata | Mikuriya | Koizumi | Koshiyama | Sonohara | Hirosue

00:15:50.190 --> 00:15:52.830
The answer is "biohazard." He's correct!

00:15:52.180 --> 00:15:54.680
<b>Q: In the process of scientific experiments, the escape of harmful 
organisms that threaten to endanger the local ecosystem is known
 by what name?  A: Biohazard</b>

00:15:52.830 --> 00:15:55.760
Mikuriya-kun, please 
distribute your three points.

00:15:56.380 --> 00:16:00.010
<i>If I take enough points off Koshiyama
 to block him from advancing,</i>

00:16:00.010 --> 00:16:01.980
<i>I'll still only have sixteen points.</i>

00:16:02.260 --> 00:16:05.480
<i>That would be the same as helping 
Shibata, now that he's catching up.</i>

00:16:05.870 --> 00:16:08.580
<i>I need to get within touching 
distance of the win first.</i>

00:16:08.580 --> 00:16:12.200
<i>If I'm confident in my speed on the 
buzzer, seventeen points is all I need.</i>

00:16:12.200 --> 00:16:13.850
<i>But nothing is absolute.</i>

00:16:13.850 --> 00:16:15.870
<i>If it was just me and Koshiyama, 
that'd be one thing,</i>

00:16:15.870 --> 00:16:17.750
<i>but Shibata and Shibuya are here, too.</i>

00:16:17.750 --> 00:16:19.910
<i>And I've already been too slow 
to buzz in several times.</i>

00:16:20.390 --> 00:16:23.340
You don't often see Mikuriya 
thinking that hard.

00:16:23.340 --> 00:16:26.880
Distributing his points here 
must be a pretty tough call.

00:16:27.170 --> 00:16:29.610
<i>There are no correct answers 
in probability theory.</i>

00:16:29.610 --> 00:16:31.120
<i>So I should...</i>

00:16:31.720 --> 00:16:33.380
This is too damn annoying.

00:16:33.380 --> 00:16:34.890
I'm keeping all three points.

00:16:35.220 --> 00:16:37.450
That gives Mikuriya-kun nineteen points.

00:16:37.450 --> 00:16:40.110
And Shiki-kun still has eighteen points.

00:16:40.110 --> 00:16:43.610
If they both get to 20 
points at the same time,

00:16:43.610 --> 00:16:46.150
the one with the higher 
total score advances, right?

00:16:46.360 --> 00:16:49.880
<i>Even if Mikuriya-kun only gets 
one point for a correct answer,</i>

00:16:49.880 --> 00:16:53.720
<i>if I buzz in and answer correctly for 
three points, I'll take the lead.</i>

00:16:55.410 --> 00:16:56.460
Koshiyama.

00:16:57.250 --> 00:16:58.740
The next question will decide it.

00:16:59.360 --> 00:17:00.020
Yeah!

00:17:00.960 --> 00:17:03.660
For better or for worse, only 
one more player can advance!

00:17:04.160 --> 00:17:05.870
The next question, please!

00:17:06.220 --> 00:17:08.500
<i>If I don't buzz this time, I could lose.</i>

00:17:08.680 --> 00:17:10.880
<i>I have to be careful... but fast!</i>

00:17:13.120 --> 00:17:13.990
Question.

00:17:14.340 --> 00:17:20.080
April 5, 1722; January 22, 1905;

00:17:20.080 --> 00:17:23.060
and May 15, 1932...

00:17:23.060 --> 00:17:24.640
<i>Three dates?</i>

00:17:24.640 --> 00:17:26.520
<i>They have something historical in common!</i>

00:17:26.520 --> 00:17:28.810
<i>Economic booms, crashes... 
Something economical?</i>

00:17:28.810 --> 00:17:30.980
<i>Celestial events... Annular solar eclipses?</i>

00:17:31.350 --> 00:17:32.610
<i>What's next?</i>

00:17:32.910 --> 00:17:35.630
All of these were, specifically, what da—

00:17:38.910 --> 00:17:39.490
<i>No!</i>

00:17:39.840 --> 00:17:40.450
<i>That little...</i>

00:17:43.670 --> 00:17:44.910
Specifically...

00:17:46.730 --> 00:17:48.500
Not again!

00:17:48.500 --> 00:17:51.880
No, it looked intentional that time.

00:17:52.350 --> 00:17:56.000
Specifically, it was... the fourteenth 
question in the first group, I believe?

00:17:56.000 --> 00:17:56.510
Oh!

00:17:57.040 --> 00:18:00.350
Continents of the world:
The "cradle of humankind"...

00:18:00.680 --> 00:18:03.240
"Specifically," as the word suggests,

00:18:03.240 --> 00:18:05.560
is a signal that what comes next 
is the core of the question.

00:18:05.960 --> 00:18:10.370
It's often used in probability questions, 
like the one about the continents.

00:18:10.370 --> 00:18:14.850
The question just now was "All of these 
were what day of the week," right?

00:18:14.850 --> 00:18:17.280
Which means it also has 
seven possible answers!

00:18:17.440 --> 00:18:21.120
Sonohara's brother predicted that 
the inclusion of "specifically"

00:18:21.120 --> 00:18:24.450
meant that, however difficult, 
it would be a probability question

00:18:24.450 --> 00:18:26.960
with possible answers that 
everyone is familiar with.

00:18:27.460 --> 00:18:29.410
<i>Everyone has a one-in-seven 
chance to answer correctly,</i>

00:18:29.410 --> 00:18:31.810
<i>even if they don't know the answer.</i>

00:18:31.810 --> 00:18:33.890
<i>In a way, it's a free question.</i>

00:18:33.890 --> 00:18:35.920
<i>He plans to rely on intuition, doesn't he?</i>

00:18:36.270 --> 00:18:38.420
<i>A one-in-seven possibility...</i>

00:18:38.420 --> 00:18:41.310
<i>It's hard to believe he'd bet 
everything on that, but...</i>

00:18:44.440 --> 00:18:47.130
<i>April 5th, January 22nd...</i>

00:18:47.130 --> 00:18:50.350
<i>The one that's most familiar 
as a date is May 15th.</i>

00:18:50.850 --> 00:18:54.150
<i>Showa 7! That has to be it.
 The May 15th Incident!</i>

00:18:54.560 --> 00:18:57.780
<i>Is there a hint within the incident 
as to what day it took place?</i>

00:18:57.780 --> 00:19:02.190
<i>No, even if I know what the incident 
was, I don't know that kind of detail!</i>

00:19:04.030 --> 00:19:07.540
<i>Thinking too long about something 
I don't know is just a waste of time!</i>

00:19:07.540 --> 00:19:09.630
<i>So I'll use math to solve it!</i>

00:19:10.890 --> 00:19:12.960
Mikuriya's writing something.

00:19:13.350 --> 00:19:14.970
<i>Zeller's congruence, huh?</i>

00:19:15.290 --> 00:19:17.460
<i>And what will Koshiyama-kun do?</i>

00:19:18.050 --> 00:19:19.910
<i>He seems to be good with history.</i>

00:19:19.910 --> 00:19:21.120
<i>Can he get it right?</i>

00:19:21.790 --> 00:19:23.200
Ten seconds.

00:19:23.200 --> 00:19:24.180
<i>Oh, no!</i>

00:19:24.640 --> 00:19:26.750
<i>Stop! Calm down! Remember!</i>

00:19:27.240 --> 00:19:28.140
<i>No...</i>

00:19:28.440 --> 00:19:29.330
<i>Think!</i>

00:19:30.160 --> 00:19:32.340
Come on, Koshiyama, what are you doing?!

00:19:32.340 --> 00:19:33.760
Time's runnin' out!

00:19:33.760 --> 00:19:34.860
Koshiyama-kun...

00:19:38.940 --> 00:19:41.580
<i>I know about the May 15th 
Incident. I read about it.</i>

00:19:42.480 --> 00:19:45.320
<i>It was one of the many books 
I read during my time spent alone.</i>

00:19:45.830 --> 00:19:49.890
<i>I'm sure the answer is buried
 in my memory somewhere.</i>

00:19:49.890 --> 00:19:51.640
<i>I have to open up to that page!</i>

00:19:55.260 --> 00:19:58.640
<i>On May 15th, Showa year 7, at about 5:30 PM,</i>

00:19:58.640 --> 00:20:00.750
<i>several young naval officers 
split up into four groups</i>

00:20:00.750 --> 00:20:02.940
<i>with the goal of overthrowing the 
corrupt political party system.</i>

00:20:02.940 --> 00:20:07.000
<i>One of those groups infiltrated the 
residence of Prime Minister Inukai Tsuyoshi.</i>

00:20:07.660 --> 00:20:09.940
<i>Even surrounded by naval 
officers in uniform,</i>

00:20:09.940 --> 00:20:12.190
<i>Prime Minister Inukai didn't panic,</i>

00:20:12.190 --> 00:20:14.860
<i>but calmly attempted to reason 
with them until his final moments.</i>

00:20:15.270 --> 00:20:18.070
<i>But he couldn't stop history's 
trigger from being pulled.</i>

00:20:20.740 --> 00:20:23.430
<i>Wait, that doesn't tell me 
what day of the week it was!</i>

00:20:23.820 --> 00:20:24.780
<i>What else is there?</i>

00:20:25.830 --> 00:20:27.670
<i>After hearing the news, 
the Prime Minister's wife</i>

00:20:27.670 --> 00:20:30.500
<i>rushed to his residence with the 
Minister for Foreign Affairs.</i>

00:20:30.500 --> 00:20:34.370
<i>Oh, right... His wife escaped 
disaster because she'd been away.</i>

00:20:34.730 --> 00:20:35.750
<i>Away?</i>

00:20:36.180 --> 00:20:40.020
<i>Where were his wife and 
son-in-law, Minister Yoshizawa?</i>

00:20:39.380 --> 00:20:40.760
<b>...were at the Imperial Hotel to celebrate...</b>

00:20:41.840 --> 00:20:45.690
<i>Wow... The Imperial Hotel 
was around back then, too?</i>

00:20:50.940 --> 00:20:54.140
<i>I'm out of time! I'm still 
not certain, but...</i>

00:20:54.370 --> 00:20:56.150
<i>I have to go with this!</i>

00:22:30.700 --> 00:22:32.740
The regular meet arc is nearing its end.

00:22:32.970 --> 00:22:35.500
I bet you're all feeling the tension!

00:22:36.010 --> 00:22:37.000
Yes!

00:22:37.300 --> 00:22:40.430
Wow! What's this place? It's so cute!

00:22:40.430 --> 00:22:42.500
Hey, Koshiyama, let's take a picture!

00:22:42.790 --> 00:22:44.000
A picture?

00:22:44.300 --> 00:22:46.270
It should be a selfie.

00:22:46.270 --> 00:22:47.510
Maybe from this angle?

00:22:47.800 --> 00:22:49.440
S-Selfie?

00:22:49.440 --> 00:22:51.910
Here we go! Strike a pose!

00:22:52.430 --> 00:22:55.520
Come on, make a cuter pose than that.

00:22:56.590 --> 00:22:58.490
This is harder than quiz bowl!
